    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mechanical Ventilation (MV) is a life saving measure routinely used in intensive care units for patients incapable of independently breathing. Ventilators function by pumping air and oxygen into patients’ lungs, taking over the function of the major respiratory muscle, the diaphragm. Because the machine does the mechanical work of breathing, the diaphragm is placed at rest.  This “disuse” causes the diaphragm to lose a significant amount of contractile force after only 12 hours of MV, leading to a clinical condition called ventilation-induced diaphragm dysfunction (VIDD) [1].  VIDD often leads to prolonged ventilator dependence, which is closely associated with the occurrence of numerous clinical complications such as pneumonia and ultimately increases mortality in patients with prolonged ventilation [1]. Some of the molecular pathways underlying MV-induced diaphragm dysfunction have been elucidated; oxidative stress is believed to be one of the most important pathways [2]. Reducing oxidative stress with, e.g., use of an antioxidant, is thus a potential therapeutic approach to prevent VIDD and thus reduce ventilator dependence in patients.  Thioredoxin-2 (TRX2) is a gene that encodes a mitochondrial-resident antioxidant protein, Trx2.  Trx2 regulates redox homeostasis by reducing the levels of H2O2, thus preventing oxidative damage to cellular proteins and reducing cell death [3]. Trx2 has already been shown to provide protection against oxidative stress-induced damage in mouse heart muscle [2,3]. I hypothesized that transgenic mice overexpressing Trx2 protein would be able to overcome MV-induced oxidative stress and that they thereby would not develop, or develop less severe, VIDD. This hypothesis was tested by performing 12-hour MV in both wild type (WT) and TRX2-trangenic mice, immediately followed by isometric contractile force measurements of the diaphragm muscle to assess Trx2’s effect on diagram muscle contractility. With the limited number of mice available, much of the data failed to reach significance; however, we did see an increase in force generation by the ventilated TRX2 mouse diaphragms compared to ventilated WT mouse diaphragms. This data justifies further investigation to verify if TRX2 mice are, in fact, protected from the ventilation-associated reduction in contractile force, and how Trx2 might be delivered exogenously as a potential therapy for ventilator-induced diaphragm dysfunction.",
